window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["hugging-face"]=[{"id":"hugging-face-f0001","card":"q-hugging-face-001-1-setup","sec":"hugging-face-s002","q":"What can you reuse from the Hugging Face Hub instead of building it yourself, and why does that shorten the model lifecycle?","a":"<p>You can reuse tokenizers, pretrained model checkpoints and labeled datasets. The tokenizer and the base model already encode a lot of training work, so you skip data collection and base training and go straight to using or fine-tuning the model. Models and datasets are downloaded once and cached locally (default cache is under <code>~/.cache/huggingface</code>), so later runs start fast and can work offline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0002","card":"q-hugging-face-001-1-setup","sec":"hugging-face-s002","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, what does <code>transformers.logging.set_verbosity_error()</code> do, and what is the risk of calling it everywhere?","a":"<p>It lowers the library's log level so only errors are printed. This hides noisy warnings and info messages. The risk is that it also hides useful warnings, such as the message that a classification head was newly initialized and must be trained. Use it in demos and notebooks, but keep warnings on while developing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0003","card":"q-hugging-face-001-1-setup","sec":"hugging-face-s002","q":"Why should a Hugging Face project pin exact versions of <code>transformers</code>, <code>datasets</code> and <code>evaluate</code> in <code>requirements.txt</code>?","a":"<p>APIs and defaults change between releases (for example default models for a pipeline task, TensorFlow support, argument names), so unpinned installs can silently change behavior or break. Pinning makes runs reproducible across machines and CI. Trade-off: you must upgrade deliberately and re-test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0004","card":"q-hugging-face-001-1-setup","sec":"hugging-face-s002","q":"Which extra packages does a Hugging Face workflow typically need beyond <code>transformers</code> when using T5 tokenizers and the ROUGE metric?","a":"<p><code>sentencepiece</code> is needed for tokenizers built on SentencePiece, such as T5. <code>rouge_score</code> (and <code>nltk</code> for sentence splitting) is needed by <code>evaluate.load(\"rouge\")</code>. <code>evaluate</code> itself hosts the metric loaders. A missing package shows up as an ImportError when you load the tokenizer or metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0005","card":"q-hugging-face-002-2-question-answering","sec":"hugging-face-s003","q":"What is extractive question answering, and what inputs does the model need?","a":"<p>The model gets a question plus a context passage and returns the span of the context that best answers the question. It does not write new text. Without a context there is nothing to extract from, so the context is a required input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0006","card":"q-hugging-face-002-2-question-answering","sec":"hugging-face-s003","q":"How does an extractive QA model pick its answer internally?","a":"<p>Question and context are tokenized together. The model scores every token as a possible start and a possible end of the answer, and the best valid start/end pair gives the span. The pipeline maps token positions back to character positions in the context and reports a confidence score.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0007","card":"q-hugging-face-002-2-question-answering","sec":"hugging-face-s003","q":"How do open-domain and closed-domain question answering differ, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Open domain covers any topic over a huge corpus (public search), so it needs larger models, costs more and is less accurate. Closed domain targets one area (product FAQs, pharma) with a limited corpus, so smaller models work, accuracy is higher and cost is lower. Choose closed domain for enterprise self-service where the knowledge base is known. Choose open domain only when users can ask about anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0008","card":"q-hugging-face-002-2-question-answering","sec":"hugging-face-s003","q":"What architecture is typically used for open-domain QA when the corpus is too large to feed to the model at once?","a":"<p>A retriever-reader design. A retriever (keyword search or dense vector search) narrows the corpus to a few relevant passages, and an extractive reader model finds the answer span in them. Feeding the whole corpus to the reader is impossible because of the model's input length limit and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0009","card":"q-hugging-face-002-2-question-answering","sec":"hugging-face-s003","q":"A user asks a question whose answer is not in the supplied context. What goes wrong with an extractive QA model, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>A model trained only on answerable questions still returns some span, which is a wrong answer. Use a model trained with unanswerable questions (SQuAD 2.0 style) and enable <code>handle_impossible_answer=True</code> in the <code>question-answering</code> pipeline so it can return an empty answer. Also filter on the confidence score.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0010","card":"q-hugging-face-003-qa-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s004","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, how do you create a question-answering pipeline for a specific model, and how do you call it?","a":"<p>Build it with <code>pipeline(\"question-answering\", model=\"&lt;model id&gt;\")</code>, then call it with <code>question=</code> and <code>context=</code> keyword arguments. The first call downloads the model into the local cache. Example: <code>qa = pipeline(\"question-answering\", model=\"deepset/minilm-uncased-squad2\")</code> then <code>qa(question=\"...\", context=\"...\")</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0011","card":"q-hugging-face-003-qa-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s004","q":"What fields does a Hugging Face <code>question-answering</code> pipeline return, and what does each mean?","a":"<p>A dict with <code>answer</code> (the extracted text), <code>score</code> (confidence between 0 and 1), and <code>start</code> and <code>end</code> (character offsets of the span in the context). The offsets let you highlight the answer in the original text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0012","card":"q-hugging-face-003-qa-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s004","q":"A QA pipeline returns an answer with <code>score</code> 0.26 for one question and 0.95 for another. How should the application use this?","a":"<p>Treat the score as a confidence signal. Set a threshold tuned on a labeled validation set, return answers that score at or over it, and fall back (ask for clarification, route to a human, say \"I don't know\") for the rest. The score is not a calibrated probability, so choose the threshold empirically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0013","card":"q-hugging-face-003-qa-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s004","q":"Why does the <code>question-answering</code> pipeline struggle with a context longer than the model's input limit, and how does it cope?","a":"<p>Transformer models accept a fixed maximum number of tokens (384 for the pipeline's default <code>max_seq_len</code>). The pipeline splits long contexts into overlapping windows (overlap set by <code>doc_stride</code>), runs the model on each window and keeps the best-scoring span. The overlap prevents an answer from being cut at a window boundary. Very long documents are still slow, so retrieve relevant passages first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0014","card":"q-hugging-face-003-qa-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s004","q":"What does the model <code>deepset/minilm-uncased-squad2</code> tell you from its name?","a":"<p>It is a MiniLM model (a small distilled transformer) with an uncased vocabulary, fine-tuned on SQuAD 2.0 (which includes unanswerable questions) and published by deepset. Small size means fast CPU inference. Uncased means it lowercases text, so capitalization is ignored.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0015","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"Why is plain accuracy a poor metric for question answering, and which metrics does SQuAD use instead?","a":"<p>A predicted answer can be partly right (\"Paris is one of the best cities\" vs \"Paris\"), so right or wrong loses information. SQuAD reports Exact Match (prediction equals a reference after normalization) and token-level F1 (overlap of predicted and reference words, balancing precision and recall).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0016","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"How do Exact Match and F1 differ for a QA answer, and when does each matter?","a":"<p>Exact Match is all or nothing: 100 if the normalized strings match, else 0. F1 gives partial credit based on shared tokens. If the reference is \"Paris\" and the prediction is \"Paris is one of the best cities in the world\", Exact Match is 0 and F1 is greater than 0 but low because precision is poor. Use Exact Match when you need strict correctness, F1 to see how close answers are.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0017","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"In the Hugging Face <code>evaluate</code> library, how do you compute the SQuAD v2 metric, and what format must predictions and references follow?","a":"<p>Load it with <code>evaluate.load(\"squad_v2\")</code> and call <code>compute(predictions=..., references=...)</code>. A prediction is a dict with <code>id</code>, <code>prediction_text</code> and <code>no_answer_probability</code>. A reference is a dict with <code>id</code> and <code>answers</code>, where <code>answers</code> holds <code>text</code> and <code>answer_start</code> lists. The <code>id</code> values must match between the two lists, and a wrong format raises an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0018","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"What is the difference between the <code>squad</code> and <code>squad_v2</code> metrics in Hugging Face <code>evaluate</code>?","a":"<p><code>squad_v2</code> supports unanswerable questions: predictions carry <code>no_answer_probability</code>, and results include <code>HasAns_*</code> and <code>NoAns_*</code> breakdowns plus best-threshold values. <code>squad</code> (v1.1) assumes every question has an answer. Use the version that matches how your evaluation data was built.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0019","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"What keys does the <code>squad_v2</code> result contain, and what does <code>HasAns_f1</code> tell you?","a":"<p>It returns <code>exact</code>, <code>f1</code> and <code>total</code>, with <code>HasAns_*</code> and <code>NoAns_*</code> versions and best-threshold variants when the data has both kinds of questions. <code>HasAns_f1</code> is the F1 only over questions that do have an answer, so it shows span-extraction quality separately from how well the model refuses unanswerable questions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0020","card":"q-hugging-face-004-squad-metric","sec":"hugging-face-s005","q":"Why compute SQuAD metrics over a whole evaluation set instead of a few examples, and how do you build that set?","a":"<p>A few examples are noisy and cannot show regressions. Build a set of contexts, questions and correct answers that reflects real traffic, run the model on it, and compare predictions with references using the metric. Re-run it whenever you change the model, threshold or preprocessing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0021","card":"q-hugging-face-005-3-text-summarization","sec":"hugging-face-s006","q":"How do extractive and abstractive summarization differ, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Extractive summarization scores sentences and copies the top ones verbatim. It is simpler and stays faithful to the source text, but can miss information and read choppily. Abstractive summarization writes new sentences that capture the meaning, so it reads more naturally and compresses better, but it is harder to build and can hallucinate facts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0022","card":"q-hugging-face-005-3-text-summarization","sec":"hugging-face-s006","q":"When would you choose extractive over abstractive summarization?","a":"<p>Choose extractive when faithfulness matters (legal, medical, compliance text), when you need cheap and predictable output, or when you cannot risk invented content. Choose abstractive when readability and compact rewording matter more, such as previews or executive briefs, and add human review or fact checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0023","card":"q-hugging-face-005-3-text-summarization","sec":"hugging-face-s006","q":"What is BART and why is it suited to summarization?","a":"<p>BART (Bidirectional and Auto-Regressive Transformers, from Facebook/Meta) is an encoder-decoder model. The encoder is bidirectional like BERT and reads the whole input. The decoder is autoregressive like GPT and writes the output token by token. It is pretrained by corrupting text and learning to reconstruct it, which suits sequence-to-sequence tasks such as summarization, generation and translation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0024","card":"q-hugging-face-005-3-text-summarization","sec":"hugging-face-s006","q":"Which common use cases need text summarization?","a":"<p>Long documents, meeting or call transcripts, news and article previews, email thread previews and support ticket digests. The goal is a short text that keeps the context and key points.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0025","card":"q-hugging-face-005-3-text-summarization","sec":"hugging-face-s006","q":"Why do encoder-decoder models like BART and T5 handle summarization better than an encoder-only model like BERT?","a":"<p>Summarization maps one sequence to a different, shorter sequence, so it needs a decoder that produces tokens. BERT is an encoder that outputs representations, so it is used for classification, tagging or extractive QA. An encoder-only model can support extractive summarization by scoring sentences, but it cannot write new text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0026","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"In Hugging Face Transformers, how do you run summarization with <code>pipeline</code>, and what does <code>min_length</code> and <code>max_length</code> control?","a":"<p>Use <code>pipeline(\"summarization\", min_length=10, max_length=100)</code> and call it with the text; the result is a list of dicts with a <code>summary_text</code> key. <code>min_length</code> and <code>max_length</code> bound the summary length in tokens, not words or characters. Tune them per use case, because a long forced minimum can make the model pad or repeat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0027","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"If you call <code>pipeline(\"summarization\")</code> without naming a model, what happens, and why is that risky?","a":"<p>The library picks a default checkpoint for the task (historically <code>sshleifer/distilbart-cnn-12-6</code>). The default can change between library versions, so output changes with an upgrade. In production, always pass <code>model=</code> explicitly and pin the library version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0028","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"What is <code>sshleifer/distilbart-cnn-12-6</code>, and what do the numbers mean?","a":"<p>It is DistilBART, a distilled version of BART fine-tuned on the CNN/DailyMail news dataset. The 12 and 6 are the encoder and decoder layer counts, so the decoder was cut in half versus BART-large's 12. Fewer layers give faster, smaller inference at a small quality cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0029","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"Why clean the input text (for example remove stray line feeds) before sending it to a summarization model?","a":"<p>The model sees raw characters as tokens. Odd line breaks, markup or boilerplate waste tokens against the input limit and can lead to broken sentences in the output. Normalize whitespace and strip formatting first, which is cheap and often improves quality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0030","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"How do you handle a document that is longer than a summarization model's maximum input length?","a":"<p>BART-style models accept about 1024 tokens. Split the document into chunks, summarize each, then summarize the concatenated summaries (map-reduce style), or use a long-context model. Truncation silently drops the end of the document, so do not rely on it. Chunk on paragraph or sentence boundaries to avoid cutting ideas.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0031","card":"q-hugging-face-006-summarization-pipeline","sec":"hugging-face-s007","q":"How can you inspect which checkpoint and architecture a Hugging Face pipeline loaded?","a":"<p>Read the model config from the pipeline, for example <code>pipe.model.config</code>. It shows the model type, architecture class (such as <code>BartForConditionalGeneration</code>), layer counts and other hyperparameters, and <code>config.name_or_path</code> shows the checkpoint id. This confirms that a default model was not swapped in unexpectedly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0032","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"What does ROUGE measure, and why is accuracy a poor fit for summaries?","a":"<p>ROUGE (Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation) measures n-gram or sequence overlap between a produced summary and a reference text, scored from 0 to 1. Many different summaries can be correct, so exact-match style accuracy punishes valid wording. ROUGE gives graded credit for overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0033","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"How do ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, ROUGE-L and ROUGE-Lsum differ?","a":"<p>ROUGE-1 counts unigram (word) overlap. ROUGE-2 counts bigram overlap, so it reflects phrase-level wording. ROUGE-L uses the longest common subsequence, which captures word order without needing adjacent matches. ROUGE-Lsum computes ROUGE-L at sentence level (splitting on newlines) and combines it, which suits multi-sentence summaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0034","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"How do you compute ROUGE with the Hugging Face <code>evaluate</code> library?","a":"<p>Load it with <code>evaluate.load(\"rouge\")</code> and call <code>compute(predictions=[summary], references=[reference])</code>. Both are lists of strings, one entry per example. It returns a dict with <code>rouge1</code>, <code>rouge2</code>, <code>rougeL</code> and <code>rougeLsum</code>. It needs the <code>rouge_score</code> package installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0035","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"What are the limits of ROUGE as a summarization metric?","a":"<p>It measures surface overlap, not meaning, so a faithful paraphrase scores low and a fluent summary with a wrong fact can score high. It does not detect hallucination. It also depends on the quality of the reference. Combine it with embedding-based metrics, factuality checks or human review for abstractive summaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0036","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"What does ROUGE-1 score if a summary equals the reference exactly, and if the two share no words?","a":"<p>Exact equality gives 1.0 on all ROUGE variants. No shared words gives 0.0. Real summaries land in between, and there is no universal good value, so compare against a baseline on your own data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0037","card":"q-hugging-face-007-rouge-score","sec":"hugging-face-s008","q":"How should you use ROUGE in practice to pick a quality bar for a summarization product?","a":"<p>Score a sample of summaries against references, read them to see which score ranges look acceptable, and set a threshold from that. Then track ROUGE on production traffic samples and use it to compare models or settings such as <code>min_length</code>/<code>max_length</code>. Treat the number as a trend signal, not as proof of quality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0038","card":"q-hugging-face-008-4-natural-language-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s009","q":"What is natural language generation, and which transformer parts does it use?","a":"<p>NLG produces human-like text from a phrase to several paragraphs. It relies on the transformer decoder, which predicts one token at a time conditioned on earlier tokens. Models include decoder-only GPT-style models and encoder-decoder models such as BART and T5.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0039","card":"q-hugging-face-008-4-natural-language-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s009","q":"Which business use cases fit NLG?","a":"<p>Content creation, chatbots and voice bots, translation, personalized emails, text reports from structured data, and product descriptions. In each case a model writes the text, and a human or rule-based check should review risky output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0040","card":"q-hugging-face-008-4-natural-language-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s009","q":"What are the main risks of using text generation in production, and how do you reduce them?","a":"<p>Hallucinated facts, inconsistent tone, unsafe or biased content, and non-deterministic output. Reduce them by grounding the prompt in retrieved data, constraining output length and format, using safety filters and evaluation sets, fixing seeds or lowering sampling randomness where repeatability matters, and keeping humans in the loop for high-stakes text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0041","card":"q-hugging-face-008-4-natural-language-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s009","q":"What is the difference between decoder-only and encoder-decoder models for generation, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Decoder-only models (GPT-2, Qwen) continue a prompt, so they suit open-ended generation and chat. Encoder-decoder models (BART, T5) read an input fully with the encoder and write a new sequence with the decoder, so they suit tasks that map input to output such as translation and summarization. Modern large chat models are mostly decoder-only, but encoder-decoder models stay efficient for narrow transformation tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0042","card":"q-hugging-face-009-content-generation-gpt-2","sec":"hugging-face-s010","q":"How do you produce several different continuations from the same prompt with a Hugging Face <code>text-generation</code> pipeline?","a":"<p>Pass <code>num_return_sequences=N</code> when calling the pipeline, and make sure decoding is not greedy. Sampling (<code>do_sample=True</code>) gives N different continuations. Beam search needs <code>num_beams &gt;= num_return_sequences</code> and returns the N best beams, which are often near-identical. Plain greedy decoding (<code>do_sample=False</code>, <code>num_beams=1</code>) raises a <code>ValueError</code> when N is greater than 1. The <code>gpt2</code> checkpoint ships task defaults that turn sampling on for text generation, which is why it works without extra flags.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0043","card":"q-hugging-face-009-content-generation-gpt-2","sec":"hugging-face-s010","q":"In a Hugging Face <code>text-generation</code> pipeline, what is the difference between <code>max_new_tokens</code> and <code>max_length</code>?","a":"<p><code>max_new_tokens</code> limits only the tokens the model adds. <code>max_length</code> limits the total length of prompt plus output, so a long prompt leaves little room for generation. Prefer <code>max_new_tokens</code> because its meaning does not change with prompt length. If both are set, <code>max_new_tokens</code> wins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0044","card":"q-hugging-face-009-content-generation-gpt-2","sec":"hugging-face-s010","q":"What does <code>transformers.set_seed(1)</code> do for text generation, and what does it not guarantee?","a":"<p>It seeds Python, NumPy and the deep learning framework random generators so sampled output repeats on the same setup. It does not guarantee identical output across different library versions, hardware or batch settings, and it has no effect on greedy decoding (which is already deterministic).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0045","card":"q-hugging-face-009-content-generation-gpt-2","sec":"hugging-face-s010","q":"Why does GPT-2 produce different text on each run, and how would you make it more or less random?","a":"<p>When sampling, each next token is drawn from a probability distribution, so runs differ. Lower <code>temperature</code> or <code>top_k</code>/<code>top_p</code> to make output more focused and repeatable, raise them for more variety. Setting <code>do_sample=False</code> gives deterministic greedy decoding, which tends to be repetitive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0046","card":"q-hugging-face-009-content-generation-gpt-2","sec":"hugging-face-s010","q":"Why is GPT-2 considered a decoder-only model, and what does that mean for how you use it?","a":"<p>GPT-2 has only the transformer decoder stack with causal (left-to-right) attention. It continues a text prompt and has no separate encoder for an input sequence. You use it for prompt continuation and, with careful prompting or fine-tuning, for other text tasks. It has no instruction tuning, so it does not follow instructions well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0047","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"Why does a chatbot built with a Hugging Face <code>text-generation</code> pipeline need the full conversation history on every call?","a":"<p>The model is stateless. It only sees the text passed to it, so earlier turns must be included for follow-up questions to make sense. Append each user message and each assistant reply to a list and send the whole list each turn. Trade-off: the prompt grows with every turn, so you eventually need truncation or summarization of old turns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0048","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"In a chat message list for a Hugging Face text-generation pipeline, what are the roles <code>system</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>assistant</code> for?","a":"<p>Each message is a dict with <code>role</code> and <code>content</code>. <code>system</code> sets the persona and rules for the bot, <code>user</code> carries what the person said, and <code>assistant</code> carries the model's earlier replies. For recent Transformers versions (v4.4x and later), the pipeline accepts this list directly and applies the model's chat template to format it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0049","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"In a loop that calls a Transformers chat pipeline, what goes wrong if the commented-out <code>chat_history.append(reply)</code> line is left disabled?","a":"<p>Without appending the assistant reply to <code>chat_history</code>, the next call sees only the user messages, so the bot forgets what it said and follow-ups lose context. Each turn must add the reply (<code>chat_history.append(reply)</code>) so history alternates user and assistant.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chat_history = [{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;system&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;You are a helpful bot&quot;}]\nfor text in [&quot;Hi&quot;, &quot;Tell me a joke&quot;]:\n    chat_history.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: text})\n    outputs = conv_pipeline(chat_history, max_new_tokens=512)\n    reply = {&quot;role&quot;: &quot;assistant&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;&lt;last message of the pipeline output&gt;&quot;}\n    # chat_history.append(reply)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"hugging-face-f0050","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"How do you load a chat model and tokenizer explicitly and pass them to a Hugging Face pipeline, and when is that useful?","a":"<p>Load them with <code>AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name)</code> and <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)</code>, then call <code>pipeline(\"text-generation\", model=model, tokenizer=tokenizer)</code>. It is useful when you need to customize loading (dtype, device placement, quantization, a tokenizer change) or reuse the same objects in several places.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0051","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"Why do real conversational bots combine several NLP services instead of using one generation model?","a":"<p>Different tasks need different strengths. Named entity recognition extracts values such as an order number, sentiment analysis detects frustration, question answering looks up facts, and text generation writes the reply. Combining them gives structured, checkable steps, for example extract the order number, call the backend, then let the model phrase the answer from real data. It also reduces hallucination.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0052","card":"q-hugging-face-010-conversation-generation","sec":"hugging-face-s011","q":"What is an instruction-tuned model, and why use one for a chatbot instead of a base model such as GPT-2?","a":"<p>An instruction-tuned model was further trained on prompt and response pairs (and often preference data), so it follows instructions, answers questions and stays in a chat format. A base model only predicts a plausible continuation and may ramble or ignore the question. For assistants, pick an instruct/chat model. Small ones such as 0.5B parameters run on CPU but are weaker at reasoning and facts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0053","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"Why is machine translation done with context-aware models instead of word-for-word lookup?","a":"<p>Word order, grammar, idioms and word meaning depend on context, and languages differ in gender, tense and structure. Transformer models use attention over the full sentence so they translate meaning, not individual words.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0054","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"What makes machine translation hard to deploy?","a":"<p>Many languages and regional variants, and large models that need much memory. One option is a smaller custom model for a narrow language pair or domain. Quality varies by language pair, and low-resource languages have less training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0055","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"How does a single T5 model perform translation, summarization and question answering?","a":"<p>T5 treats every task as text-to-text. A task prefix at the start of the input tells it what to do, such as <code>translate English to German: ...</code> or <code>summarize: ...</code>. The same model, loss and decoding are used for all tasks. This works because the original T5 was trained on a multi-task mix with those prefixes. A prefix the model never saw may give poor results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0056","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"When would you use <code>AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM</code> and <code>AutoTokenizer</code> directly instead of <code>pipeline(\"translation\")</code>?","a":"<p>Use them when you need control over tokenization or decoding, when the pipeline does not support a tokenizer and model combination, or when you want to reuse loaded objects. Trade-off: you write pre-processing (tokenize, tensors) and post-processing (decode) yourself, while the pipeline hides both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0057","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"In this T5 snippet, what do <code>return_tensors=\"pt\"</code> and <code>skip_special_tokens=True</code> do?","a":"<p><code>return_tensors=\"pt\"</code> makes the tokenizer return PyTorch tensors, which <code>model.generate</code> needs (use <code>\"tf\"</code> for TensorFlow). <code>skip_special_tokens=True</code> removes markers such as <code>&lt;pad&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/s&gt;</code> from the decoded text so you get clean output.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(&quot;translate English to French: Hello&quot;, return_tensors=&quot;pt&quot;)\noutputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=40)\nprint(tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"hugging-face-f0058","card":"q-hugging-face-011-machine-translation","sec":"hugging-face-s012","q":"What does <code>model.generate(inputs[\"input_ids\"], max_length=40)</code> do for a seq2seq model, and what happens if the translation needs more tokens?","a":"<p>It runs autoregressive decoding: the decoder produces one token at a time until it emits an end token or reaches <code>max_length</code>. If the translation needs more tokens than the limit, the output is cut off mid-sentence, so set the limit to fit the longest expected output (or use <code>max_new_tokens</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0059","card":"q-hugging-face-012-5-transfer-learning-sentiment-model","sec":"hugging-face-s013","q":"What is transfer learning in NLP, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>You start from a model pretrained on a huge text corpus and fine-tune it on a small task-specific dataset, usually training a new classification head on top. It solves the cost of training from scratch: you need far less data, compute and time (minutes instead of days) and often get better accuracy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0060","card":"q-hugging-face-012-5-transfer-learning-sentiment-model","sec":"hugging-face-s013","q":"When fine-tuning a pretrained transformer, when do you freeze layers and when do you retrain everything?","a":"<p>Freeze the encoder (train only the new head) when data is small, compute is limited or you want to keep general knowledge and avoid overfitting. Unfreeze some or all layers when you have more data or the domain differs a lot from pretraining, usually with a small learning rate. Full fine-tuning gives the best accuracy but costs more and risks forgetting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0061","card":"q-hugging-face-012-5-transfer-learning-sentiment-model","sec":"hugging-face-s013","q":"Which steps make up a transfer-learning workflow for text classification with Hugging Face?","a":"<p>Load the dataset, tokenize and encode it with the checkpoint's tokenizer, load the base model with a new classification head, optionally freeze layers, train (compile and fit, or a trainer), evaluate on validation data, then run inference with the same pre-processing. Each step uses the same checkpoint name so tokenizer and model match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0062","card":"q-hugging-face-012-5-transfer-learning-sentiment-model","sec":"hugging-face-s013","q":"Which checkpoint family fits a text-classification task, and why was <code>distilbert-base-uncased</code> a reasonable base?","a":"<p>An encoder model suits classification because it builds a representation of the whole input. DistilBERT keeps about 97% of BERT's language understanding with roughly 40% fewer parameters and runs faster, so it is a good low-cost base for sentiment. Trade-off: slightly lower accuracy than full BERT or newer encoders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0063","card":"q-hugging-face-012-5-transfer-learning-sentiment-model","sec":"hugging-face-s013","q":"In Hugging Face, which TensorFlow classes does Transformers offer, and what is their status?","a":"<p>TensorFlow variants have a <code>TF</code> prefix, such as <code>TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification</code>. As of Transformers 4.x Hugging Face has deprecated TensorFlow and Flax support in favor of PyTorch, so for new work prefer <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification</code> with PyTorch. TensorFlow code still works on pinned versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0064","card":"q-hugging-face-013-5-1-load-the-dataset","sec":"hugging-face-s014","q":"How do you load a dataset from the Hugging Face Hub, and what does the result look like?","a":"<p>Call <code>datasets.load_dataset(\"&lt;name&gt;\")</code>. It returns a <code>DatasetDict</code> keyed by split (typically <code>train</code>, <code>validation</code>, <code>test</code>), where each split is a <code>Dataset</code> with named columns. Data is stored in Apache Arrow format and cached locally after the first download.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0065","card":"q-hugging-face-013-5-1-load-the-dataset","sec":"hugging-face-s014","q":"Why does the <code>datasets</code> library use Apache Arrow, and what benefit does that give?","a":"<p>Arrow is a columnar, memory-mapped format. Datasets larger than RAM can be read from disk without loading everything, access is fast, and <code>map</code> results are cached on disk. This makes preprocessing big text data practical.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0066","card":"q-hugging-face-013-5-1-load-the-dataset","sec":"hugging-face-s014","q":"How do you find the label names of a classification dataset loaded with <code>load_dataset</code>?","a":"<p>Read the <code>features</code> of the split: <code>ds[\"train\"].features[\"label\"].names</code> returns the list of class names, and the label column stores integer indexes into that list. Use the list to turn predicted indexes back into readable labels and to get <code>num_labels</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0067","card":"q-hugging-face-013-5-1-load-the-dataset","sec":"hugging-face-s014","q":"Why keep separate train, validation and test splits?","a":"<p>Train updates the weights. Validation is used during training to tune hyperparameters and decide when to stop. Test is used once at the end to estimate real-world performance. Using test data for tuning leaks information and gives an optimistic estimate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0068","card":"q-hugging-face-013-5-1-load-the-dataset","sec":"hugging-face-s014","q":"How do you bring your own data (a list or dict in memory) into a Hugging Face workflow?","a":"<p>Convert it with <code>Dataset.from_dict({...})</code> (or <code>load_dataset</code> with a <code>csv</code> or <code>json</code> loader for files). Optionally wrap splits in a <code>DatasetDict</code>. After that you can use <code>map</code>, tokenization and conversion to framework datasets exactly as with a Hub dataset.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0069","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"What do <code>input_ids</code> and <code>attention_mask</code> contain after a Hugging Face tokenizer runs?","a":"<p><code>input_ids</code> are integer token IDs that index the model's embedding matrix. <code>attention_mask</code> has 1 for real tokens and 0 for padding, so the model ignores padding positions. Both have the same length per example.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0070","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"For BERT-style tokenizers such as DistilBERT's, what are IDs 101 and 102 and 0 in <code>input_ids</code>?","a":"<p>In the uncased BERT vocabulary, 101 is <code>[CLS]</code> (start of the sequence, its output is used for classification), 102 is <code>[SEP]</code> (end of the sequence), and 0 is <code>[PAD]</code>, which fills shorter inputs up to the batch length. These IDs are specific to that vocabulary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0071","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"In a Hugging Face tokenizer call, what do <code>padding=True</code> and <code>truncation=True</code> do and why are both needed?","a":"<p>Models take rectangular batches, so <code>padding=True</code> pads shorter texts to the same length (the longest in the batch). <code>truncation=True</code> cuts texts longer than the model's maximum length (512 tokens for BERT-style models), because longer input would fail. Padding is hidden from the model through the attention mask.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0072","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"In the Hugging Face <code>datasets</code> library, what does <code>dataset.map(tokenize, batched=True, batch_size=None)</code> do when <code>tokenize</code> is your own function that calls the tokenizer, and why use those arguments?","a":"<p><code>map</code> applies the function to every split. <code>batched=True</code> passes batches of rows to the function so the fast tokenizer can process many texts at once. <code>batch_size=None</code> makes each whole split a single batch, so padding goes to the longest text in that split and every example has the same length. Trade-off: very large datasets may need a smaller batch size to save memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0073","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"Why must you use the tokenizer that matches the model checkpoint, and how do you pick the class?","a":"<p>The vocabulary and special tokens must match what the model was trained with. Use <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(checkpoint)</code>, which selects the right class (for example the DistilBERT tokenizer) automatically. A mismatch produces wrong IDs and bad predictions without an obvious error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0074","card":"q-hugging-face-014-5-2-tokenize-and-encode","sec":"hugging-face-s015","q":"Why is padding every example to the dataset's longest text wasteful, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>Short texts get many padding tokens, which wastes compute. Dynamic padding pads each batch only to its own longest example, for example with <code>DataCollatorWithPadding</code>. It speeds up training and inference at no accuracy cost. Fixed padding is simpler and fine for small data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0075","card":"q-hugging-face-015-5-3-model-architecture-and-freezing","sec":"hugging-face-s016","q":"What happens when you call <code>from_pretrained(checkpoint, num_labels=N)</code> for a sequence classification model?","a":"<p>It loads the checkpoint's architecture and pretrained weights, then adds a new classification head with N outputs. The head weights are randomly initialized, so Transformers prints a warning that they must be trained. <code>num_labels</code> sets the size of that final layer, so it must equal the number of classes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0076","card":"q-hugging-face-015-5-3-model-architecture-and-freezing","sec":"hugging-face-s016","q":"What does freezing a layer mean, and why do it when fine-tuning?","a":"<p>A frozen layer's weights are not updated by backpropagation. Freezing the pretrained encoder keeps its learned language knowledge, speeds up training, needs less memory for gradients and lowers overfitting risk on small datasets. The cost is lower task-specific accuracy than full fine-tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0077","card":"q-hugging-face-015-5-3-model-architecture-and-freezing","sec":"hugging-face-s016","q":"In a Keras model, how do you freeze the first layer, and what must you remember afterward?","a":"<p>Setting <code>trainable = False</code> on a layer stops its weights from being updated. In Keras you must compile the model again after changing <code>trainable</code>, or the change may not take effect in training. Check with <code>model.summary()</code>, which lists trainable and non-trainable parameter counts.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.layers[0].trainable = False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"hugging-face-f0078","card":"q-hugging-face-015-5-3-model-architecture-and-freezing","sec":"hugging-face-s016","q":"In a Transformers sequence classification model built on DistilBERT, which layer holds most of the parameters, and which layers are new?","a":"<p>The DistilBERT encoder main layer holds nearly all parameters (about 66 million of about 67 million). The <code>pre_classifier</code> and <code>classifier</code> dense layers are tiny and are the task-specific head that training must learn. This is why freezing the encoder leaves only a very small number of trainable parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0079","card":"q-hugging-face-015-5-3-model-architecture-and-freezing","sec":"hugging-face-s016","q":"In a Transformers Keras model such as <code>TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification</code>, why does reading <code>model.get_config()</code> help before fine-tuning?","a":"<p>It shows the architecture's hyperparameters such as the number of layers, attention heads, hidden size and dropout. You can confirm you loaded the intended base model and its size, and decide whether to shrink it by removing layers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0080","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"What are the three steps to train a Keras-based Hugging Face model on a tokenized dataset?","a":"<p>Convert the dataset to a TensorFlow dataset with <code>to_tf_dataset</code>, call <code>compile</code> with the optimizer, loss and metrics, then call <code>fit</code> with training data, validation data and epochs. It is ordinary Keras training with the pretrained weights as the starting point.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0081","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"In the datasets library, what do <code>columns</code>, <code>label_cols</code>, <code>shuffle</code> and <code>batch_size</code> do in <code>to_tf_dataset</code>?","a":"<p><code>columns</code> lists the model input columns to feed (for example the tokenizer's <code>model_input_names</code> such as <code>input_ids</code> and <code>attention_mask</code>), <code>label_cols</code> names the label column, <code>shuffle</code> randomizes row order each epoch, and <code>batch_size</code> sets the batch size. Shuffle the training set, not the validation set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0082","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"Why is <code>from_logits=True</code> set in <code>SparseCategoricalCrossentropy</code> when fine-tuning a Transformers classifier?","a":"<p>The model outputs raw logits, not probabilities. With <code>from_logits=True</code> the loss applies softmax internally in a numerically stable way. If you leave it false while passing logits, the loss is computed on wrong values and training suffers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0083","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>SparseCategoricalCrossentropy</code> and <code>CategoricalCrossentropy</code>?","a":"<p>Sparse categorical cross-entropy expects integer class labels (0, 1, 2, 3). Categorical cross-entropy expects one-hot vectors. Datasets that store labels as class indexes use the sparse version, which avoids converting to one-hot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0084","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"Why is a small learning rate such as 5e-5 used to fine-tune a pretrained transformer?","a":"<p>The pretrained weights are already good. Large updates can destroy that knowledge (catastrophic forgetting) and make training unstable. Fine-tuning usually uses 1e-5 to 5e-5 with Adam or AdamW. Too small a rate trains slowly or stalls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0085","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"Training accuracy rises each epoch (59%, 84%, 91%, 94%) while validation accuracy peaks near 87% and then drops to 83%. What is happening and what do you do?","a":"<p>The model is overfitting: it memorizes training data and generalizes worse. Stop at the best epoch with early stopping (for example the Keras <code>EarlyStopping</code> callback with <code>restore_best_weights=True</code>), add regularization or dropout, get more data, freeze more layers or lower the learning rate. Always pick the model by validation metrics, not training metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0086","card":"q-hugging-face-016-5-4-train","sec":"hugging-face-s017","q":"How do you decide the number of epochs when fine-tuning a transformer?","a":"<p>Fine-tuning needs few epochs (often 2 to 5) because the model starts well trained. Watch validation loss and accuracy each epoch and stop when they stop improving. Use early stopping rather than a fixed number if you can.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0087","card":"q-hugging-face-017-5-5-predict-with-the-custom-model","sec":"hugging-face-s018","q":"When you skip the <code>pipeline</code> and use a fine-tuned model directly, what pre-processing and post-processing must you do yourself?","a":"<p>Pre-processing: tokenize the text with the same tokenizer and settings used in training and convert to tensors (or a framework dataset) in the same format. Post-processing: take the model's logits and turn them into a label, typically with argmax and the label-name list. The pipeline does both for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0088","card":"q-hugging-face-017-5-5-predict-with-the-custom-model","sec":"hugging-face-s018","q":"In a Transformers TensorFlow sequence-classification model (such as <code>TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification</code>), what does <code>predictions.logits</code> contain after <code>model.predict(...)</code>, and how do you turn it into a predicted label?","a":"<p>Logits are raw, unnormalized scores, one per class for each input (shape: examples by classes). Take <code>np.argmax(logits, axis=1)</code> to get the class index, then map the index to a name with the dataset's label names. Apply softmax first only if you need probabilities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0089","card":"q-hugging-face-017-5-5-predict-with-the-custom-model","sec":"hugging-face-s018","q":"What is the pitfall of passing <code>shuffle=True</code> to <code>to_tf_dataset</code> when building an inference dataset?","a":"<p>Shuffling changes the row order, so predictions no longer line up with the original texts and labels. Use <code>shuffle=False</code> for inference and evaluation so output index i matches input i.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0090","card":"q-hugging-face-017-5-5-predict-with-the-custom-model","sec":"hugging-face-s018","q":"A fine-tuned sentiment model predicts the right label for one of two sample inputs. What is wrong with judging the model from that?","a":"<p>Two samples are far too few. Evaluate on a held-out test set big enough to be representative and report accuracy plus per-class metrics (precision, recall, F1) because classes may be imbalanced. For wrong cases, look at label quality, class balance and domain mismatch, then add data or retrain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0091","card":"q-hugging-face-017-5-5-predict-with-the-custom-model","sec":"hugging-face-s018","q":"How do you save a fine-tuned model so another service can load it later?","a":"<p>Call <code>model.save_pretrained(path)</code> and <code>tokenizer.save_pretrained(path)</code>, then load both with <code>from_pretrained(path)</code>, or push them to the Hub. Save the tokenizer too, because inference needs the identical tokenization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0092","card":"q-hugging-face-018-inference-challenges","sec":"hugging-face-s019","q":"What makes serving transformer models in production hard?","a":"<p>Model fit (a checkpoint trained on other data may be inaccurate for yours), size (billions of parameters need lots of memory), heavy compute (GPUs parallelize well but cost more), latency (seconds per request), scaling many concurrent requests, and cost per inference.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0093","card":"q-hugging-face-018-inference-challenges","sec":"hugging-face-s019","q":"A transformer endpoint is too slow and too expensive. What would you check and change?","a":"<p>Measure first: latency per request, batch size and hardware use. Then reduce model cost with a smaller or distilled model, quantization or pruning, batch requests, cache repeated inputs, move to GPU or an optimized runtime (such as ONNX Runtime or TensorRT), and scale replicas with autoscaling. Retest accuracy after each change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0094","card":"q-hugging-face-018-inference-challenges","sec":"hugging-face-s019","q":"Why does model size matter beyond disk space when you deploy a transformer?","a":"<p>Weights plus temporary activations must fit in RAM or GPU memory for each replica. A bigger model forces bigger and costlier instances, fewer replicas per machine, slower loading (cold starts) and more latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0095","card":"q-hugging-face-018-inference-challenges","sec":"hugging-face-s019","q":"How do you serve many concurrent requests to a single transformer deployment?","a":"<p>Scale out with multiple replicas behind a load balancer and autoscaling, use dynamic batching so the GPU processes several requests together, keep models loaded in memory, and use asynchronous queues for non-interactive work. Batching raises throughput but adds some latency per request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0096","card":"q-hugging-face-019-when-to-customize-a-pretrained-model","sec":"hugging-face-s020","q":"When should you fine-tune a pretrained model instead of using it as it is?","a":"<p>When accuracy is too low out of the box, when your domain has its own vocabulary or grammar (medical, legal, social media, regional language), or when you want a smaller model. First measure the baseline on your own data, because a pretrained model is often good enough and customization adds cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0097","card":"q-hugging-face-019-when-to-customize-a-pretrained-model","sec":"hugging-face-s020","q":"What best practices apply to transfer learning on a small dataset?","a":"<p>Use a small, specific dataset that covers all scenarios evenly, make sure labels are accurate and the data is high quality, change the architecture one step at a time and measure each change, validate across scenarios, monitor production for drift and bias, and retrain when new data arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0098","card":"q-hugging-face-019-when-to-customize-a-pretrained-model","sec":"hugging-face-s020","q":"What alternatives exist to fine-tuning when a pretrained model underperforms?","a":"<p>Prompting or few-shot examples for generative models, retrieval-augmented generation to add knowledge, a different or larger checkpoint, and post-processing rules. Fine-tuning is the right choice when behavior, style or domain language must change and you have good labeled data. RAG is better when the issue is missing or changing facts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0099","card":"q-hugging-face-019-when-to-customize-a-pretrained-model","sec":"hugging-face-s020","q":"Why monitor a fine-tuned model in production for drift?","a":"<p>Real input changes over time (new vocabulary, topics, user behavior), so accuracy falls even though the model did not change. Track input statistics and quality metrics, and retrain on fresh labeled data when they degrade.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0100","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"Why compress a trained model, and what are the two broad approaches?","a":"<p>To cut memory, math operations and compute so inference is cheaper and faster. The two routes: reduce the number of layers, nodes and parameters, or store values in smaller types (for example FLOAT32 to integers).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0101","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"How do quantization and pruning differ?","a":"<p>Quantization lowers numeric precision (for example 32-bit floats to 8-bit integers), shrinking memory and speeding up math on supporting hardware. Pruning removes weights, nodes or connections that contribute little. Quantization usually gives easier gains and is supported by most frameworks. Pruning needs sparse-aware runtimes to translate into real speedups, and often retraining.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0102","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"What is knowledge distillation, and how did it produce DistilBERT?","a":"<p>A small student model is trained to imitate a large teacher model's outputs (soft probabilities) rather than only the hard labels. The student retains much of the teacher's ability with fewer parameters. DistilBERT is a distilled BERT with about 40% fewer parameters and faster inference. Trade-off: it needs a training run and expertise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0103","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"In what order should a team try model compression techniques?","a":"<p>Start with the simple ones: quantization and pruning (and picking an already distilled checkpoint). Try distillation, low-rank approximation and neural architecture search only if they are not enough, because they need more time, compute and expertise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0104","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"What must you do after compressing a model before releasing it?","a":"<p>Re-run the model on the original validation and test data and compare accuracy, latency and memory with the uncompressed baseline. Compression can lower accuracy, especially on rare classes, so confirm the loss is acceptable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0105","card":"q-hugging-face-020-model-compression","sec":"hugging-face-s021","q":"What is low-rank approximation, and what does neural architecture search do?","a":"<p>Low-rank approximation replaces a large weight matrix with the product of smaller ones, dropping information that matters little. Neural architecture search automatically explores model structures to find the best one for a problem. It is very compute intensive, so it is rarely the first choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0106","card":"q-hugging-face-021-serving-multiple-models","sec":"hugging-face-s022","q":"What are the three patterns for combining several models in one solution?","a":"<p>Chain (sequential): one model's output feeds the next, such as intent detection then entity extraction then question answering. Parallel: the same input goes to several independent models at once and results are collected, such as sentiment, topics and abuse detection on a review. Alternate (conditional): a condition selects one model, such as a language detector choosing the right translator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0107","card":"q-hugging-face-021-serving-multiple-models","sec":"hugging-face-s022","q":"When would you use a parallel pattern instead of a chain for multiple models?","a":"<p>Use parallel when the models do not depend on each other's output. It cuts total latency to roughly that of the slowest model instead of the sum. Use a chain only when a later step needs an earlier result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0108","card":"q-hugging-face-021-serving-multiple-models","sec":"hugging-face-s022","q":"What risks come with a chain of models, and how do you control them?","a":"<p>Errors compound (a wrong intent breaks the later steps) and latency adds up. Validate that each model's output suits the next one, exit early when confidence is low (for example intent confidence too low), test the models together as one solution and measure end-to-end latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0109","card":"q-hugging-face-021-serving-multiple-models","sec":"hugging-face-s022","q":"How do you scale a system that serves several models?","a":"<p>Deploy and scale each model independently so a heavy model gets more replicas without over-provisioning light ones. Use a router or orchestration layer to chain, fan out or select models, and monitor each model's latency, errors and accuracy separately plus the whole pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0110","card":"q-hugging-face-021-serving-multiple-models","sec":"hugging-face-s022","q":"Which pattern fits \"detect the language, then translate to English with the matching translator\", and why?","a":"<p>The alternate (conditional) pattern. A detector picks which translation model runs, so only one translator runs per request, which saves compute compared with running all translators.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0111","card":"q-hugging-face-022-models-used","sec":"hugging-face-s023","q":"How do encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder models map to typical Hugging Face tasks?","a":"<p>Encoder-only (BERT, DistilBERT, MiniLM) fits classification, tagging and extractive QA. Decoder-only (GPT-2, Qwen) fits open-ended text generation and chat. Encoder-decoder (BART, T5) fits summarization and translation. Choose by whether the output is a label, a span or new text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0112","card":"q-hugging-face-022-models-used","sec":"hugging-face-s023","q":"Why prefer a small distilled checkpoint (DistilBERT, DistilBART, MiniLM) for a first deployment?","a":"<p>It is smaller and faster, cheaper to serve and often close in accuracy, so you can reach a working service quickly. Move to a larger model only if measured accuracy on your data is not enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0113","card":"q-hugging-face-022-models-used","sec":"hugging-face-s023","q":"Why does the Hub model name matter when you build a pipeline, and what should you check before choosing one?","a":"<p>The id encodes the architecture and the training data. Check the model card for task, training data, language, license, size and reported metrics, and test on your own data. A model trained for English news may fail on your domain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0114","card":"q-hugging-face-023-pipeline-task-names","sec":"hugging-face-s024","q":"What does the <code>task</code> string passed to <code>pipeline(...)</code> decide?","a":"<p>It selects the pre-processing, the model head class and the post-processing for that task, for example <code>\"question-answering\"</code>, <code>\"summarization\"</code>, <code>\"text-generation\"</code>, <code>\"sentiment-analysis\"</code> (alias of <code>\"text-classification\"</code>) and <code>\"translation_xx_to_yy\"</code>. Passing a model that does not fit the task gives wrong output or an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0115","card":"q-hugging-face-023-pipeline-task-names","sec":"hugging-face-s024","q":"How do you run translation in Transformers?","a":"<p>Use <code>pipeline(\"translation_en_to_de\", model=...)</code> for a supported pair, or load a seq2seq model such as T5 with its tokenizer and add the task prefix to the input text, then call <code>generate</code>. The second route gives more control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0116","card":"q-hugging-face-023-pipeline-task-names","sec":"hugging-face-s024","q":"What happens if you call <code>pipeline(\"sentiment-analysis\")</code> with no model, and how do you make it production-safe?","a":"<p>The library loads a default sentiment checkpoint (a DistilBERT fine-tuned on SST-2 at the time of writing, which can change between versions). Name the model explicitly and pin versions, or use your own fine-tuned model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0117","card":"q-hugging-face-024-evaluation-metrics","sec":"hugging-face-s025","q":"Which metric do you use to evaluate extractive QA, summarization and a classifier, and why?","a":"<p>QA: SQuAD Exact Match and F1, because answers are spans that can be partly right. Summarization: ROUGE, because it measures overlap with a reference summary. Classification: accuracy, plus precision, recall and F1 per class (especially with imbalanced classes). Each metric matches how outputs of that task can be right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0118","card":"q-hugging-face-024-evaluation-metrics","sec":"hugging-face-s025","q":"What is the role of the Hugging Face <code>evaluate</code> library, and how do you use it?","a":"<p>It provides a common interface for metrics. Load one with <code>evaluate.load(\"&lt;metric name&gt;\")</code> and call <code>compute(predictions=..., references=...)</code>. The required prediction and reference formats differ per metric, so read the metric's documentation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0119","card":"q-hugging-face-024-evaluation-metrics","sec":"hugging-face-s025","q":"Why track metrics both offline and in production for an NLP model?","a":"<p>Offline metrics on a fixed evaluation set compare models and catch regressions before release. Production monitoring catches data drift and real user impact that the test set does not show. Use both, and refresh the evaluation set over time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0120","card":"q-hugging-face-025-three-api-levels","sec":"hugging-face-s026","q":"What are the three levels of Hugging Face Transformers APIs, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Pipeline API: one call for inference with built-in pre- and post-processing, best for fast prototypes and simple needs. AutoTokenizer plus AutoModel: you control tokenization, model call and decoding, best for custom logic and batching. Fine-tuning with a task model class such as <code>TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification</code> (or the <code>Trainer</code> in PyTorch): best when you must train on your own data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0121","card":"q-hugging-face-025-three-api-levels","sec":"hugging-face-s026","q":"What is the difference between <code>AutoModel</code> and <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification</code>?","a":"<p><code>AutoModel</code> loads the base transformer and returns hidden states, with no task head. <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification</code> adds a classification head on top and returns logits. Use the task class when you need predictions or fine-tuning, <code>AutoModel</code> when you need embeddings or features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0122","card":"q-hugging-face-025-three-api-levels","sec":"hugging-face-s026","q":"Why do <code>Auto*</code> classes exist, and what do they save you?","a":"<p>They read the checkpoint's config and pick the right concrete model or tokenizer class, so you write <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)</code> and need not know whether it is a BERT, T5 or GPT tokenizer. Code stays the same when you swap checkpoints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"hugging-face-f0123","card":"q-hugging-face-025-three-api-levels","sec":"hugging-face-s026","q":"Why use a pipeline for a prototype but the lower-level APIs in production?","a":"<p>A pipeline is quick, but it hides batching, device placement, padding and decoding choices. With the lower-level APIs you control those, which helps with throughput, latency and cost, and you can plug in custom pre- and post-processing. Many teams still keep pipelines when performance is adequate.</p>","hand":true}];
